{'gap_id': 'GAP-033', 'category': 'SW', 'priority': 'P1', 'status': 'OPEN', 'skill': 'governance', 'section': None, 'description': "TERM ADJUDICATION HAS NEVER RUN, AND NOTHING SAYS SO. Harvest at evidence, adjudicate at judgment (owner 2026-08-27, overruling DR-2026-08-24 section 2.4). The harvest half works -- derive it with: select count(*) from observed_terms. The adjudication half has produced nothing at all -- select count(*) from term_adjudications -- and db.py adjudicate-term exists, so this is a backlog, not a missing capability. CLAUDE.md section 6 says outright that zero links AFTER judgment is a defect, and no gap, no check and none of the five documents a cold-starting session reads names this. Found by walking the spine forward from this batch's two admissions: research -> evidence walks, evidence -> judgment stops here with no marker. WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: adjudicate the harvested terms, or record why the backlog is deliberate. WHAT WOULD MAKE IT VISIBLE MEANWHILE: a check asserting that observed_terms older than N batches have an adjudication, which is the general form of the same question. || CORRECTED 2026-09-25: BATCH 20: ADJUDICATION HAS RUN OVER THE WHOLE BACKLOG, AND THIS GAP STAYS OPEN ONLY FOR WHAT WAS DEFERRED WITH A REASON. Every observed_terms row now carries a term_adjudications row, each with an observation-specific rationale. DERIVE THE COUNTS, DO NOT QUOTE THEM: select outcome, count(*) from term_adjudications group by outcome; and the residual backlog: select count(*) from observed_terms o where not exists (select 1 from term_adjudications a where a.observation_id=o.observation_id). SIX TERMS WERE MINTED through add-term (NAMES-NEW), each warranted by the corpus rather than coined: ramp (the element; TERM-001 is its slope), level difference, ramp run length, intermediate landing, ramp usability (the outcome side: completion, Borg effort, discomfort and difficulty ratings are adjudicated to it as measures), and accessibility audit -- derive them with select term_id, canonical_en from terms where created_by_session like '%batch-20%'. THE DEFERRALS GROUP INTO FIVE OPEN QUESTIONS, and each is a decision about the vocabulary, not about a phrase: (1) RAMP VERSUS ROUTE GRADIENT -- TERM-001 is defined on ramps, four observations measure path and trail slopes, and extraction 49 already files REF-01003's path slopes on parameter 3; this is a scoping question for parameter 3 itself and belongs to the batch that re-determines it. (2) ACTIVITY PHRASES (ascending slopes, slope propulsion) -- term rows or ICF d4 links; the only activity-shaped terms are the functional-axis rows the owner called a bad coinage on 2026-08-18. (3) CONCEPTS HELD AS COLUMN VOCABULARIES (self-propulsion as device_class, CBPR as co1_source_type) -- rule 5 forbids a second home as a term, and NOT-OURS would be false. (4) THE DESIGN CASE (marginal population) -- the project's answer is the owner's most-accommodating rule, a doctrine, which a term would smuggle into the vocabulary. (5) SINGLE-SOURCE OR BACK-TRANSLATION RISKS (accessible route from a Japanese statute, site path, dwelling setting, a named housing standard, universal accessibility from a title, ramp incident, exterior ramp threshold) -- each needs a source that states the concept in its own words. PREDICTION TESTED: prior P5a (a writer or vocabulary defect surfaces before the verb runs cleanly) was NOT borne out -- adjudicate-term and add-term ran cleanly on every row. || CORRECTED 2026-09-25: CORRECTION TO THE BATCH-20 NOTE ABOVE, SAME BATCH. It groups the deferrals into five questions, and the groups as listed account for all but one: observation 54 ('walking and reaching limitations', REF-01005's title) is a FUNCTIONAL-LIMITATION phrase whose only terms are the functional-axis rows TERM-031 and TERM-041 -- not an activity phrase, which is what group (2) names. It belongs with group (2) under a wider heading: phrases whose only possible targets are the functional-axis terms the owner called a bad coinage on 2026-08-18. Derive the membership rather than trusting either note: select o.observation_id, o.surface_form from observed_terms o join term_adjudications a using (observation_id) where a.outcome='DEFERRED' order by 1. || CORRECTED 2026-09-25: OWNER RULING 2026-09-25, AND WHAT IT DID NOT SETTLE. TERM-089 ('ramp') is narrowed to exclude the running slope of paths, walkways and trails, so it no longer pre-decides group (1), the ramp-versus-route question, which stays OPEN here. THREE FURTHER POINTS that an independent adversarial pass raised on batch 20's minted terms (finding #14) are NOT resolved by that ruling and are open on this gap: (a) TERM-094 ('accessibility audit') has a scope note that states as a universal -- 'An audit measures COMPLIANCE with a yardstick, never what the yardstick should be' -- what is one testable prior (batch 20's P1b) drawn from two sources; (b) TERM-093 ('ramp usability') is defined as 'the outcome a ramp gradient is judged against' and its scope note adjudicates the measures to it as operationalisations, which forecloses a separate term for the acceptability CRITERION as distinct from the measure; (c) TERM-092's scope note says 'It bounds TERM ramp run length from above' -- a dangling reference with no term id. Each is a vocabulary decision for the owner, not a wording fix, and none was edited.", 'created_at': '2026-09-18 22:45', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining', 'updated_at': '2026-09-25 06:10', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-09-25-research-batch-20-audit-cluster-templer-fhwa', 'falsification_condition': None, 'confidence_interval': None, 'shift_conditions': None, 'named_dissenter': None, 'mining_addressability': None}

