{'candidate_id': 109, 'found_under_slug': 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'title': 'Four architectural movement studies for the wheelchair and ambulant disabled. Part 3 - Ramp gradients (1971)', 'why_not_admitted': 'HYPOTHESIS, AND POSSIBLY THE ROOT OF THE 1:12 LINEAGE. A 1971 architectural movement study whose Part 3 is titled Ramp gradients, cited by the 1997 study that tested the ADA-permitted range. If the modern codes trace to a measurement study, this is a strong candidate for where. NO DOI, so unreachable by every DOI-keyed method this project runs -- resolution needs a library or archive route, which is a different kind of work from a Crossref chase and should be planned as such rather than attempted opportunistically.', 'resolved_ref_id': None, 'notes': ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING, BUT IT IS NO LONGER A HYPOTHESIS — the record is now verified and the staged description was WRONG ON THE AUTHOR. Batch 17 staged it from REF-00980 deposited reference list as "Walter F." Open Library OL4653562M (retrieved this session) gives: "Four architectural movement studies for the wheelchair and ambulant disabled", by_statement "[studies directed by] Felix Walter.", published by the DISABLED LIVING FOUNDATION, 346 Kensington High St, London, 1971, 60 pp. R15 says correct an over-claim rather than let a guess harden: the CORPORATE author is the Disabled Living Foundation and Walter directed the studies. Neither the reference list nor the catalogue is wrong; they name different things, and the staged row named only one. WHAT THE FULL TEXT WOULD SETTLE, AND WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN ITS TIER: REF-01005 page 163 reports that Walter and Steinfeld INDEPENDENTLY found 1:16 over 20 feet accessible to at least 95 percent of wheelchair users. That convergence is currently held on Steinfeld testimony alone, so Walter is a single document counted once, not twice. Retrieving it would make the convergence real. NOT RETRIEVED: no digitised copy found via Open Library, Internet Archive or Google Books; HathiTrust returned a Cloudflare interstitial and is unresolved rather than negative. NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED: Jisc Library Hub Discover, and the Disabled Living Foundation successor organisation. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW RETRIEVAL CLAIM, same defect as candidate 110. The re-description above says "no digitised copy found via Open Library, Internet Archive or Google Books". GOOGLE BOOKS WAS NEVER ASKED — HTTP 429, daily quota exceeded. Open Library and Internet Archive stand and were re-derived. The bibliographic record, which is what this row is FOR, is unaffected: Open Library OL4653562M, Disabled Living Foundation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 UPDATE: THE NAMED NEXT ROUTE IS NOW CLOSED, AND THE ROW GAINS THE HOLDINGS LAYER THAT MAKES DOCUMENT DELIVERY POSSIBLE. This row\'s previous re-description ended \'NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED: Jisc Library Hub Discover, and the Disabled Living Foundation successor organisation.\' JISC WAS TRIED THIS BATCH AND IS UNREACHABLE: two queries, both HTTP 403 carrying a Cloudflare \'Just a moment...\' interstitial, both persisted. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT JISC HOLDS -- blocked route, not a null -- and it is the second discovery catalogue in two batches to fail this way after HathiTrust. The row should stop naming Jisc as its next step. NOTHING IS RETRACTED FROM THE EARLIER DESCRIPTION: I checked whether batch 18 had missed the Open Library record before writing anything, and it had not -- Felix Walter, DLF, London, 1971, 60 pp is already correctly recorded above, and the corporate-versus-directing-author distinction it draws stands. WHAT IS NEW IS THE IDENTIFIER SET, from the same Open Library edition record OL4653562M re-read this batch: ISBN-10 0901908053, LCCN 77502052, OCLC 222208, LC classification NA2545.P5 D5, Dewey 729/.2, one edition only, publisher address 346 Kensington High St, London W.14, source record marc:marc_loc_2016 -- a LIBRARY OF CONGRESS MARC record, which is why the 1971 date is authoritative rather than inferred, and which independently corroborates the year REF-01005\'s Table 13 footnote c gives while that bibliography entry\'s own year digits are OCR-damaged. WHY THE IDENTIFIERS ARE THE POINT: GAP-026 says what this needs is a library or document-delivery route and that this is \'a different kind of work from a retrieval ladder\'. An OCLC number and an LCCN are exactly what converts that from a search into a request -- interlibrary loan on OCLC 222208, or a direct approach to a holding library. NO SEARCH THIS ENVIRONMENT CAN RUN WILL PRODUCE THE TEXT, and this row should not be handed to another retrieval battery. A NEW SEARCH ROUTE DID WORK AND IS WORTH KEEPING AS METHOD: batch 18 searched Open Library by TITLE and got 0; searching by PUBLISHER, publisher:\'Disabled Living Foundation\', returns 99 records and the target is among them. Query shape, not index coverage (R14). STILL THE SAME REASON TO WANT IT: the 1:16-over-20-feet convergence is held on Steinfeld\'s testimony about Walter, so it is one document counted once until Walter itself is read. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, AND NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, applied literally: \'if we can\'t access something from anywhere then we have to give up on it for now.\' Full text of Walter 1971 is not reachable from anywhere this project can go. ROUTES RECORDED AS TRIED, in this row and in batches 17-19\'s retrieval logs: Open Library (the bibliographic record OL4653562M, no digitised copy; by title 0, by publisher 99 with the record among them); Internet Archive (no copy); HathiTrust (Cloudflare interstitial); Jisc Library Hub Discover (HTTP 403, Cloudflare, twice); Google Books (quota refused, never asked; quota value 0). The 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports re-checking every legitimate self-service route and finding none that works; its requests were not persisted in this repository\'s retrieval log, so they are not itemised here. THE EARLIER NEXT STEP IS WITHDRAWN: batch 19 closed this row by naming a request-based route with the catalogue identifiers it had found; under the ruling that route is not a task for any person, human or agent, and nothing in this row should be read as one. THE BIBLIOGRAPHIC RECORD STANDS (Disabled Living Foundation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter). WHAT THE PROJECT HOLDS OF WALTER INSTEAD: two independent second-hand accounts -- REF-01005 page 163 (extraction 54) and, new this batch, REF-01008 printed page 4 (extraction 64): an adjustable indoor laboratory ramp, 20 ft at 1:8.6 to 1:16 and 10 ft at 1:5.5 to 1:10, four-point rating scale, recommending for self-propelled wheelchairs 1:9 over 10 ft and 1:16 over 20 ft. Two readers agreeing about Walter is still Walter counted once. Left PENDING-VERIFICATION rather than retired because the column vocabulary has no \'exhausted\' value; the disposition should be read with this note. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): DISPOSITION MOVED TO EXHAUSTED. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, recorded on contact (CLAUDE.md rule 0): a real, CHECK-permitted value for \'exhausted, given up for now\' was added (schema migration 096) and applies to this row, to which no route exists from anywhere. The routes tried and the reasons are in the re-description above; this changes the typed column so that no sweep reads PENDING-VERIFICATION as work. Not queued for anyone.'}
{'candidate_id': 110, 'found_under_slug': 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'title': 'A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps (1957)', 'why_not_admitted': 'HYPOTHESIS. The oldest ramp-specification study in any reference list this corpus holds. Same locator problem as the 1971 study and the same reason to want it: a specification study from 1957 is either the origin of a threshold still in force or evidence that the threshold was never measured at all, and both answers matter to GAP-002.', 'resolved_ref_id': None, 'notes': ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING AND ITS DESCRIPTION IS NOW CORROBORATED BY A PRIMARY SOURCE RATHER THAN BY A REFERENCE LIST. Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0. BUT REF-01005 page 163 characterises it directly: "Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users. However, his sample was taken from wheelchair users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education center and the findings probably reflect the high standard of excellence in rehabilitation training that the subjects received as part of their program." SO THE 1957 FINDING IS 1:8, AND THE FIELD OWN 1979 ASSESSMENT IS THAT IT IS UNREPRESENTATIVE BECAUSE THE SAMPLE WAS ELITE-TRAINED. That is a selection-bias critique published eight years before the ADA, and it is itself first-class historical evidence about how the threshold literature was weighed. A WEB SEARCH SUMMARY ASSERTED THIS WAS A UNIVERSITY OF IOWA MASTER THESIS AND NONE OF ITS OWN RETURNED LINKS SUPPORTED THAT — the claim is discarded, not recorded (CLAUDE.md 5(c)). The institution is NOT established: REF-01005 says only "a pioneering rehabilitation-education center" and naming one would be invention. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW OWN RETRIEVAL CLAIM. The re-description above states "Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0". GOOGLE BOOKS 0 IS FALSE. That query returned HTTP 429 (daily quota exceeded) and was never asked; recording an unfired query as a null is the error batch 17 made on SafetyLit and is corrected here rather than left standing. THE OTHER THREE ZEROS HOLD and were re-derived, not assumed: Open Library numFound 0, Internet Archive 0 relevant, ERIC 0. EVERYTHING ELSE IN THE ROW IS UNAFFECTED — Elmer 1957 finding of 1:8 maximum, and Steinfeld 1979 dismissal of it as an elite-trained sample, come from REF-01005 page 163 and stand on a retrieved primary source. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 RE-DESCRIPTION FROM A PRIMARY SOURCE, AND THE ROW GAINS AN AUTHOR IT NEVER HAD. Staged from REF-00980\'s Crossref reference list as a bare title with a year, \'A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps (1957)\'. REF-01005 supplies three things the reference list did not, from two different places in the same document. (1) THE AUTHOR AND THE FORM, from its bibliography at pypdf page index 167: \'Elmer, C.D. [A s]t[udy] to determine the s[p]e[ci]fications of wheelchai[r] ramp[s]. Ph.D. d[is]ser[ta]tion, University o[f] Il[linois]\'. NOTE HONESTLY: the bibliography\'s own year digits are OCR-damaged and read \'194-5/\', which is NOT 1957 and is not resolvable from those bytes. (2) THE YEAR, from the body text at page index 162, which reads \'Elmer (1957)\' cleanly -- so 1957 is corroborated by a primary source, but by the prose, not by the reference entry. (3) THE FINDING, which is why this row now matters more than it did: REF-01005 reports that \'Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users\' and then DISCOUNTS IT ON SAMPLING -- his subjects were young, highly trained users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education centre, where Walter\'s sample and Steinfeld\'s own included large proportions of older people and many with reduced arm function and low stamina. Captured as extraction 57 with contested=1. WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE ROW\'S VALUE: staged as a hypothesis that the oldest ramp-specification study might be the origin of a threshold still in force. It is not that. Its figure is 1:8, which no code carries, and the 1979 report set it aside forty-seven years ago for a reason this project would give today. THE HISTORICAL TRAJECTORY IS THE FINDING: 1:8 (1957, discounted as unrepresentative) -> 1:12 (ANSI, measured and found wanting in 1979) -> 1:16 (Walter 1971 and Steinfeld 1979 converge) -> 1:20 (every wheelchair user in Steinfeld\'s sample completed 40 feet). THE PARAMETER HAS MOVED IN ONE DIRECTION, GENTLER, FOR SEVENTY YEARS, AND EACH STEP WAS DRIVEN BY WIDENING THE SAMPLE. NOT RETRIEVED AND STILL OWED as a document: a 1957 Illinois PhD dissertation. ProQuest Dissertations and the University of Illinois institutional repository are the routes and neither has been tried. NOT AN ANCHOR IF RETRIEVED: owner ruling 2026-09-18 places pre-1990 work as historical grounding. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION, TWO PARTS, ONE OF WHICH OPENS A CONTEST THIS ROW MUST NOT CLOSE. (1) THE YEAR WAS ALWAYS PRINTED. My earlier re-description said the bibliography year digits read "194-5/" and were "NOT resolvable to 1957", and that 1957 came only from the body text. The rendered page reads "Elmer, C.D. A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps. Ph.D. dissertation, University of IL, 1957." Author, form, institution and year are all printed in the reference entry. The elaborate distinction I drew between what the bibliography could support and what the prose could support was an artefact of reading a damaged text layer instead of the page. (2) THE AUTHOR IS NOW CONTESTED BETWEEN TWO PRIMARY SOURCES, and this is new evidence rather than a correction of mine. Templer FHWA-RD-79-3 (candidate 125) cites the same 1957 Illinois dissertation as "DIXON, Charles E. A study to determine the Specifications of Wheelchair Ramps, Unpublished Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Illinois, Champagne, Urbana, 1957", and describes its method -- a campus questionnaire, then an adjustable laboratory ramp at 1:8.6 to 1:5, 61 subjects, 30 feet in a single run or two 15-foot runs with a landing. Steinfeld bibliography plainly prints Elmer, C.D. TWO 1979-80 FEDERAL REPORTS DISAGREE ON THE AUTHOR OF THE SAME DISSERTATION. Neither is corrected from the other and NEITHER IS PREFERRED HERE. My earlier note said this row "gains an author it never had"; it gains two, and that should not be read as settled. THE FINDING THE ROW CARRIES IS UNAFFECTED: whoever wrote it, REF-01005 reports its 1:8 result and discounts it on sampling, and FHWA independently reports 61 subjects who were university students, which is the same objection from a second reader. RESOLUTION: ProQuest Dissertations or the University of Illinois repository, searched on TITLE rather than author, since the author is exactly what is in doubt. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, AND NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, applied literally: \'if we can\'t access something from anywhere then we have to give up on it for now.\' Full text of the 1957 University of Illinois dissertation is not reachable from anywhere this project can go. ROUTES RECORDED AS TRIED in this row and in batches 17-19\'s retrieval logs: Open Library 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0; Google Books refused on quota and never asked. The 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports re-checking every legitimate self-service route and finding none that works; its requests were not persisted in this repository\'s retrieval log, so they are not itemised here. THE EARLIER NEXT STEP IS WITHDRAWN: batch 19 named two repository routes as \'the routes\'; under the ruling this row is not a task for any person, human or agent. WHAT STANDS: the 1:8 finding and its 1979 discount on sampling (REF-01005 extraction 57), and the AUTHOR CONTEST, now read on rendered pages of both sources: REF-01005\'s bibliography prints \'Elmer, C.D.\'; REF-01008\'s reference list (printed page 299, transcribed from the render and persisted) prints \'Dixon, Charles E. A study to determine the Specifications of Wheelchair Ramps, Unpublished Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Illinois, Champagne, Urbana, 1957.\' REF-01008 also gives the method as it read it -- a campus questionnaire, then an adjustable laboratory ramp from 1:8.6 to 1:5, 61 subjects, all university students, a 30 ft run or two 15 ft runs with a landing, a five-point scale; recommended \'A maximum gradient of 1:6 ... and a minimum of 1:8.6\', while at 1:6 47 percent rated it hard or very hard. Neither author spelling is preferred. The contest stays open because the document that would settle it is the one given up on. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): DISPOSITION MOVED TO EXHAUSTED. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, recorded on contact (CLAUDE.md rule 0): a real, CHECK-permitted value for \'exhausted, given up for now\' was added (schema migration 096) and applies to this row, to which no route exists from anywhere. The routes tried and the reasons are in the re-description above; this changes the typed column so that no sweep reads PENDING-VERIFICATION as work. Not queued for anyone.'}

